  "notes": [
    "The data bus is not in order on the package: pins 7 to 15 run D4, D3, D5, D6, VCC, D2, D7, D0, D1. Wire it from the numbers, never by eye.",
    "Unlike the 6502 the Z80 has a separate I/O address space. IORQ picks I/O, MREQ picks memory, and both are qualified with RD or WR.",
    "RFSH plus MREQ marks the DRAM refresh cycle the CPU runs during every opcode fetch. With static RAM you can ignore it, which is what most new builds do.",
    "M1 low marks an opcode fetch. M1 with IORQ is the interrupt acknowledge cycle, not an I/O read.",
    "RESET must be held low for at least three full clock cycles. The CPU then starts at address 0000.",
    "The CPU needs a single phase clock with a fast rise time and, on the original NMOS part, a pull-up to get near 5 V. The CMOS Z84C00 is happier with ordinary logic and is fully static, so you can single-step it.",
    "BUSREQ and BUSACK give you real DMA, and WAIT lets slow memory stretch a cycle. Tie INT, NMI, WAIT and BUSREQ high through resistors if you are not using them."
  ],
